{* =========================================================================
   aLatteX Demo 2 - Evolution CMS syntax, and the bridge between the two

   Every classic EVO tag is written here verbatim. aLatteX replaces each one
   with an opaque token before Latte runs and puts it back afterwards, so
   Latte never sees a brace or a bracket that belongs to Evolution CMS, and
   Evolution CMS receives its tags untouched for its own parse pass.

   Below the pass-through section, the same six things are done again through
   the Latte functions this plugin registers - the difference being that those
   take Latte expressions as arguments, so a snippet call can be assembled
   from template variables.
   ========================================================================= *}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{evoSetting('manager_language') ?: 'en'}">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <title>{$pagetitle} - aLatteX demo</title>
</head>
<body class="alx alx-evo">

{{aLatteXDemoHeader}}

<section id="passthrough">
    <h3>Classic EVO tags, passed through untouched</h3>

    {* The <dt> labels are written with HTML entities on purpose. Neither
       Latte nor Evolution CMS has a "print this tag literally" construct that
       survives both passes - {l}{r} and {syntax off} only stop Latte, and the
       EVO parser would still expand a real tag. An entity is the one form
       that reaches the browser as text. *}
    <dl>
        <dt><code>&#123;&#123;chunk&#125;&#125;</code> - HTML chunk</dt>
        <dd>{{aLatteXDemoBadge}}</dd>

        <dt><code>&#91;&#91;snippet&#93;&#93;</code> - cacheable snippet</dt>
        <dd>[[aLatteXDemoClock]]</dd>

        <dt><code>&#91;!snippet!&#93;</code> - non-cacheable snippet</dt>
        <dd>[!aLatteXDemoClock!]</dd>

        <dt><code>&#91;*field*&#93;</code> - document field or template variable</dt>
        <dd>[*pagetitle*] / [*alxSubtitle*] / [*alxTags*]</dd>

        <dt><code>&#91;(setting)&#93;</code> - system setting</dt>
        <dd>[(site_name)] on [(site_url)]</dd>

        <dt><code>&#91;+placeholder+&#93;</code> - placeholder set by a snippet</dt>
        <dd>[+alx_note+]</dd>
    </dl>

    <h4>Snippet parameters</h4>
    [[aLatteXDemoList? &items=`one||two||three` &class=`alx-passthrough`]]

    <h4>A parameter whose value is itself an EVO tag</h4>
    [[aLatteXDemoList? &items=`[*pagetitle*]||[(site_name)]` &class=`alx-mixed`]]

    <h4>Nested snippet calls</h4>
    {* aLatteXDemoNested returns EVO tags of its own; Evolution CMS resolves
       them on a later parse pass, so nesting needs nothing from aLatteX. *}
    [[aLatteXDemoNested? &depth=`2`]]

    <h4>A chunk that calls a snippet</h4>
    {{aLatteXDemoNav}}
</section>

<section id="functions">
    <h3>The same six things, as Latte functions</h3>

    {var $tagList = ['latte', 'evo', 'demo']}
    {var $listClass = 'alx-from-latte'}

    <dl>
        <dt>evoChunk()</dt>
        <dd>
            {* Renders the chunk immediately, during Latte's pass, and returns
               it as raw HTML. Whatever EVO tags the chunk contains are still
               resolved later by Evolution CMS. *}
            {evoChunk('aLatteXDemoBadge')}
        </dd>

        <dt>evoSnippet()</dt>
        <dd>
            {* Does NOT run the snippet. It writes the [[tag]] for you, so the
               snippet still goes through Evolution CMS's caching. The win over
               writing [[...]] by hand is that the arguments are Latte
               expressions. *}
            {evoSnippet('aLatteXDemoList', [
                'items' => implode('||', $tagList),
                'class' => $listClass,
                'note'  => 'Note assembled in Latte for document ' . $id,
            ])}
        </dd>

        <dt>evoUncachedSnippet()</dt>
        <dd>{evoUncachedSnippet('aLatteXDemoClock', ['format' => 'H:i:s'])}</dd>

        <dt>evoTv()</dt>
        <dd>{evoTv('alxSubtitle')} / {evoTv('pagetitle')}</dd>

        <dt>evoSetting()</dt>
        <dd>{evoSetting('site_name')} (escaped, note the ampersand)</dd>

        <dt>evoPlaceholder()</dt>
        <dd>
            {* Reads what evo()->setPlaceholder() has stored. During Latte's
               pass most placeholders do not exist yet - a snippet on this page
               sets them later - so this is mainly useful for placeholders a
               plugin published on an earlier event. *}
            {evoPlaceholder('alx_note') ?: 'not set during the Latte pass'}
        </dd>
    </dl>

    <h4>Where the functions earn their keep</h4>
    {* A snippet call that could not be written as a literal [[tag]], because
       the parameters come out of a loop. *}
    <ul>
        {foreach $tagList as $tag}
            <li>{evoSnippet('aLatteXDemoList', ['items' => $tag, 'class' => 'alx-tag-' . $tag])}</li>
        {/foreach}
    </ul>

    {* Conditional snippet choice - impossible with a bare [[tag]]. *}
    {if $published}
        {evoSnippet('aLatteXDemoClock', ['format' => 'Y-m-d'])}
    {else}
        {evoUncachedSnippet('aLatteXDemoClock')}
    {/if}
</section>

<section id="order">
    <h3>Order of operations</h3>
    <p>
        Latte runs first and Evolution CMS second. That is the single fact
        that explains every surprise on these pages: a Latte variable cannot
        depend on a snippet's output, and a snippet cannot emit Latte for the
        template to pick up.
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>aLatteX replaces every EVO tag with a token.</li>
        <li>Latte compiles and renders what is left.</li>
        <li>aLatteX puts the EVO tags back.</li>
        <li>Evolution CMS runs parseDocumentSource() over the result.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<main class="alx-content">[*content*]</main>

{{aLatteXDemoFooter}}

</body>
</html>
